      "text": "To solve the inequality\n\\[\n\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0,\n\\]\nwe need to analyze the sign of the expression on the left-hand side. The expression is a rational function, and the critical points (where the numerator or denominator is zero) are the solutions to \\(x + 6 = 0\\) and \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0\\).\n\nFirst, solve the numerator for any roots:\n\\[\nx + 6 = 0 \\implies x = -6.\n\\]\n\nNext, solve the denominator for any roots:\n\\[\nx^2 + 2x + 7 = 0.\n\\]\nMultiply both sides by 4 to complete the square:\n\\[\n4x^2 + 8x + 28 = 0 \\implies x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0.\n\\]\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is \\(2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\\), which is negative. Therefore, the quadratic equation has no real roots. This means the quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real values of \\(x\\).\n\nNow, we consider the sign of the expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\):\n- The numerator \\(-6\\) is always negative.\n- The denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive.\n\nSince the numerator is negative and the denominator is always positive, the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is always negative. The fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is impossible because it is always negative and never zero.\n\nThus, there are no \\(x\\) values that satisfy the inequality. The solution interval is the empty set.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{- \\text{No solution}}\n\\]",
